\title{X5 - proofs read lazily from the file: analysis, prototype, recommendation}
\taxon{Report}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{loader}
\tag{store}
\tag{performance}
\meta{origin}{docs/research/profiles/X5.md}
\meta{moved}{2026-11-24}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Scout X5, 2026-11-24. Question: the loader is one thread and the floor of a pipelined run (mathlib: 12.4 s native \code{\verb~|-O3~} after wave 7; 11.3 s wall with two scanner threads before it). On exporter output the proof-only records of a theorem are an id range that is recognisable without parsing them (finding F1). Can the loader leave those byte ranges in the file and the worker that checks the theorem parse them itself?}

\p{Everything here is outside the published modules: private copies and a private build under \code{\verb~|work/prof/X5/~} (\code{\verb~|build.sh~}, javac, nothing installed), two simulations on the published reader, a prototype on the real store. Living summary: \code{\verb~|work/status/prof-X5.md~}.}

\subtree{
\title{0. Result}

\p{\strong{It works and it agrees with the eager loader, but it buys much less than F1 suggested. The serial load gets 1.6 to 1.75 times faster (not five times), the workers pay 3 to 5 times the block decode for it, and today the wall clock of a pipelined run gets worse, not better.}}

\table{
\tr{\th{}\th{Init}\th{Std}\th{cedar}\th{mathlib}}
\tr{\td{expression records the loader still parses (simulation)}\td{17.9 \%}\td{21.9 \%}\td{23.4 \%}\td{27.7 \%}}
\tr{\td{bytes left to the workers}\td{76.9 \%}\td{72.1 \%}\td{71.1 \%}\td{68.7 \%}}
\tr{\td{\strong{load phase, one thread, eager -> lazy} (JVM, warm)}\td{\strong{0.52 -> 0.32 s} (x 0.61)}\td{\strong{0.93 -> 0.53 s} (x 0.57)}\td{\strong{1.43 -> 0.92 s} (x 0.64)}\td{\strong{10.64 -> 6.35 s} (x 0.60)}}
\tr{\td{\strong{proofs decoded by 8 workers, CPU, blocks -> ranges}}\td{\strong{0.16 -> 0.51 s} (x 3.2)}\td{\strong{0.23 -> 0.92 s} (x 4.0)}\td{\strong{0.31 -> 1.65 s} (x 5.3)}\td{\strong{2.65 -> 9.58 s} (x 3.6)}}
\tr{\td{- of that, the foreign records}\td{0.27 s}\td{0.52 s}\td{1.08 s}\td{4.74 s}}
\tr{\td{load + decode, CPU}\td{+23 \%}\td{+25 \%}\td{+47 \%}\td{+20 \%}}
\tr{\td{eager against lazy: every constant's type and value}\td{equal (59,979)}\td{equal (103,885)}\td{equal (151,772)}\td{equal (739,167)}}
}

\p{(JVM C2 with the launcher's flags, quiet cores, medians of warm passes; mathlib one run. Sections 1 to 5.)}

\ol{
\li{\strong{The rule holds and needs five guards.} A \code{\verb~|thm~} whose value is the last expression record of its segment and whose type is an earlier record: the records after the type are the proof. The loader keeps 18 - 28 \% of the expression records, all names and levels, and still looks at the first bytes and the end of every line. On 18,005 small files (230 arena tests, 17,775 mutants) and on Init, Std, cedar and mathlib the lazy load gives, for every constant, the same type and value as the eager load, or fails with the same message, or restarts (309 files). Without the binding guard 14 \code{\verb~|reorder_decl~} mutants load differently (sections 5.5, 6).}
\li{\strong{Foreign records are the expensive part.} 5.5 - 6.9 \% of the references of proof records go to a proof record of an earlier theorem that no shared record needed. Served by the worker from the file (binary search over 28-byte range rows, interpolation on the bytes, memo per declaration) they are 16 - 23 \% more nodes and half or more of the worker's decode time (280 - 315 ns each). A table shared by the workers would halve their number; parsing whole ranges on first use would parse 83 - 94 \% of everything again (section 2).}
\li{\strong{The serial part does not shrink with the record count.} Pass one over every line (4.0 - 4.3 GB/s), names, levels, declaration rows, page faults and the shared prefix stay. Measured lazy / eager: 0.57 - 0.64 (0.60 on mathlib: 10.6 s -> 6.4 s on the JVM); projected from the published profile of the native load of mathlib: 12.4 s -> 7.3 s, 5.7 s if a scanner thread takes pass one (section 3).}
\li{\strong{End to end} (section 5.6): under pipelining wall = max(serial loader, checking). With the machine's check phase of today (8 workers, quiet cores: Std 1.85 s, cedar 2.26 s, mathlib 42 s) checking is the larger term everywhere, so lazy ranges only add worker time: Std +6 \%, cedar +8 \%, mathlib +3 \%. They win once the check phase is shorter than the eager load, i.e. with checking 1.9 (Std), 1.6 (cedar), 3.9 (mathlib) times faster than today, and then by at most 36 - 43 \% of a loader-bound wall clock. In the check-only reading of "warm" (\code{\verb~|-X passes=N~}, later passes do not load) they can only lose.}
}

\p{\strong{Recommendation: NO-GO now; keep the design and the prototype for the day the loader is the floor.}}

\ul{
\li{Nothing of this should enter \code{\verb~|rlvm-load~} / \code{\verb~|rlvm-store~} / \code{\verb~|rlvm-driver~} while the check phase is longer than the load on the corpora that count. The switch point is measurable with numbers the team already produces: check phase with 8 workers against \code{\verb~|stop-after=load~}. Std and cedar are within a factor of two of it, mathlib a factor of four.}
\li{If it is built then, build it as section 7 says: a range is a proof block whose records are still text (no consumer of \code{\verb~|Overlay.blockRoot~} changes), restart instead of guessing, only ACCEPT final under lazy loading, and variant B for the foreign records; and cost the variant of section 7.5 first (scanner threads pack the ranges: no text in the workers, no second read, no restart). Size of the prototype: reader side 1,300 lines, loader sink 350, store decoder 600; to that come the publication of \code{\verb~|loc~} and of the range rows, the driver's restart rule and a mutation campaign aimed at ranges.}
\li{The cheaper step toward the same floor, which needs no new trust argument: \code{\verb~|-X load=pipelined~} with \code{\verb~|-X scan-threads=2~} as the default (the load path owner's proposal of 14:35Z; -21 \% wall of the load, measured by them). Pass one is context-free, so the same scanner threads could classify and count for a lazy mode later.}
\li{By-products usable now: \code{\verb~|X5Tool diff~} (structural digest of every constant of two loads of one file; it found the binding difference in minutes) and the measured unit costs for anybody who wants to cost another split: line end + class 13 ns per line, template tokenising 28 - 32 ns per record (1.7 - 2.0 GB/s), node write 20 - 35 ns, block decode 28 - 34 ns per node, a record fetched at random from the file 280 - 315 ns.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1. The rule}

\p{F1 (first half of this scout, \code{\verb~|work/prof/X5/sim/LazySim.java~}): lean4export writes the records a declaration needs right before its declaration line, children first, the type's records before the value's. With ids dense and in file order, at a \code{\verb~|thm~} / \code{\verb~|opaque~} line with type id T and value id V the records of the segment with id <= T are what the type reaches and the records with id in \verb~|(~T, V\verb~|]~ are the proof-only records; V is the last expression record of the segment. The eager loader finds the same split by parsing everything and marking what the type reaches.}

\p{The guarded rule that the prototype implements (and section 6 proves equal to the eager split):}

\blockquote{
\p{A unit is \strong{lazy} iff the reader has seen names, levels and expressions with ids 0, 1, 2, ... in file order so far, every line of the segment starts like a name, level, leaf, structure, projection or declaration record, the declaration line is a \code{\verb~|thm~} in template form, its value id V is the id of the last expression line of the segment, and its type id T is smaller than V. Then the \strong{prefix} is the expression lines with id <= T and the \strong{range} is the rest. The loader parses all name and level lines of the segment, the prefix, the \strong{leaf lines of the range} (\code{\verb~|const~}, \code{\verb~|sort~}, \code{\verb~|bvar~}, literals: they need the loader's name, level and literal tables) and the declaration line. The \strong{structure lines of the range} (\code{\verb~|app~}, \code{\verb~|lam~}, \code{\verb~|forallE~}, \code{\verb~|letE~}, \code{\verb~|proj~}) stay in the file; the loader records \code{\verb~|[first ordinal, record count, file offset, byte length]~} of the range and nothing per record.}
}

\p{Simulation of exactly this rule on the published reader (\code{\verb~|work/prof/X5/sim/LazySim2.java~}, \code{\verb~|work/prof/X5/data/lazysim2-*.txt~}; \code{\verb~|thm~} and \code{\verb~|opaque~} units; the prototype takes \code{\verb~|thm~} only, hence its slightly smaller numbers in section 5):}

\table{
\tr{\th{}\th{Init}\th{Std}\th{cedar}\th{con-leche}\th{mathlib}}
\tr{\td{expression records}\td{6,137,092}\td{9,928,514}\td{14,728,503}\td{12,221,252}\td{107,037,712}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|thm~} / \code{\verb~|opaque~} units}\td{42,695}\td{72,148}\td{83,996}\td{21,672}\td{527,437}}
\tr{\td{lazy units (= ranges)}\td{37,600}\td{64,516}\td{73,305}\td{20,093}\td{475,131}}
\tr{\td{not lazy: the value is an earlier record}\td{5,095}\td{7,632}\td{10,691}\td{1,579}\td{52,306}}
\tr{\td{not lazy: any other guard}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{\strong{the loader still parses} (prefix + eager units + leaves in ranges)}\td{17.9 \%}\td{21.9 \%}\td{23.4 \%}\td{18.6 \%}\td{\strong{27.7 \%}}}
\tr{\td{- prefix records of lazy units}\td{7.5 \%}\td{10.3 \%}\td{7.3 \%}\td{3.9 \%}\td{15.2 \%}}
\tr{\td{- records of eager units}\td{9.9 \%}\td{11.1 \%}\td{15.6 \%}\td{14.5 \%}\td{11.8 \%}}
\tr{\td{- leaf records inside ranges}\td{0.57 \%}\td{0.58 \%}\td{0.49 \%}\td{0.22 \%}\td{0.73 \%}}
\tr{\td{\strong{structure records left in the file}}\td{5.04 M (82.1 \%)}\td{7.75 M (78.1 \%)}\td{11.28 M (76.6 \%)}\td{9.94 M (81.4 \%)}\td{\strong{77.43 M (72.3 \%)}}}
\tr{\td{projections among them}\td{1,828}\td{7,068}\td{5,729}\td{7,998}\td{18,818}}
\tr{\td{promoted on demand from the file (requests / records)}\td{27,443 / 117,950}\td{42,859 / 175,923}\td{57,460 / 211,906}\td{49,123 / 169,191}\td{577,640 / 2,069,973}}
\tr{\td{- as a share of the records left}\td{2.34 \%}\td{2.27 \%}\td{1.88 \%}\td{1.70 \%}\td{2.67 \%}}
\tr{\td{(the eager loader promotes)}\td{593,925}\td{1,015,436}\td{1,503,967}\td{-}\td{9,909,017}}
}

\p{So the loader keeps 18 - 28 \% of the expression records, all 4.3 M name and level records (25 - 45 \% of them lie inside ranges), every declaration line, and one look at the first bytes and the end of every line.}
}

\subtree{
\title{2. Foreign records: how to serve them (question a)}

\p{A structure record of a range refers to: its own range 61 - 64 \%, its own prefix 0.5 - 1.7 \%, a shared term 28 - 32 \%, \strong{a structure record that an earlier range left in the file and that no shared record ever needed ("foreign") 5.5 - 6.9 \%}, a record of a proof block 0 (2 to 12 references per export, into the one ownerless block). The eager loader sees every such reference and promotes the target; the lazy loader does not, so a worker has to get the record, and what it reaches among such records, from the file.}

\table{
\tr{\th{}\th{Init}\th{Std}\th{cedar}\th{con-leche}\th{mathlib}}
\tr{\td{references of range records}\td{10.08 M}\td{15.51 M}\td{22.58 M}\td{19.92 M}\td{155.04 M}}
\tr{\td{foreign references}\td{689,829 (6.84 \%)}\td{1,044,064 (6.73 \%)}\td{1,567,050 (6.94 \%)}\td{1,330,215 (6.68 \%)}\td{8,597,279 (5.55 \%)}}
\tr{\td{ranges with any foreign record}\td{27,972 of 37,600}\td{48,948 of 64,516}\td{54,728 of 73,305}\td{15,276 of 20,093}\td{309,338 of 475,131}}
\tr{\td{\strong{A. parsed by the worker, memo per declaration}}\td{1.15 M (22.9 \%)}\td{2.13 M (27.5 \%)}\td{3.78 M (33.5 \%)}\td{2.32 M (23.3 \%)}\td{\strong{18.29 M (23.6 \%)}}}
\tr{\td{- earlier ranges entered (per declaration with any)}\td{197,761 (7.1)}\td{323,937 (6.6)}\td{394,425 (7.2)}\td{183,575 (12.0)}\td{2,473,720 (8.0)}}
\tr{\td{- records per entered range}\td{5.8}\td{6.6}\td{9.6}\td{12.6}\td{7.4}}
\tr{\td{- largest closure of one declaration}\td{3,707}\td{9,683}\td{49,757}\td{19,708}\td{21,778}}
\tr{\td{\strong{B. a table shared by all workers, keyed by record}: distinct foreign records}\td{0.50 M (10.0 \%)}\td{0.88 M (11.4 \%)}\td{1.34 M (11.9 \%)}\td{0.92 M (9.2 \%)}\td{\strong{8.36 M (10.8 \%)}}}
\tr{\td{\strong{C. whole range parsed on first use into a shared table}: ranges entered by anybody}\td{46.6 \%}\td{41.8 \%}\td{42.5 \%}\td{54.6 \%}\td{42.4 \%}}
\tr{\td{- structure records they hold}\td{83.4 \%}\td{82.7 \%}\td{85.7 \%}\td{94.4 \%}\td{83.9 \%}}
}

\p{(percentages of A and B: of the structure records left in the file.)}

\p{\strong{The index.} The record id is written in every line and the ids of a range are dense, so no per-record index is needed (4 bytes per record would be 310 MB on mathlib): the range of an ordinal is a binary search over the first ordinals of the ranges (mathlib: 475,131 ints = 1.9 MB, 19 steps), the line is found by interpolation on the bytes of the range and a short walk (\code{\verb~|RangeFile.find~}): measured 1.02 - 1.07 interpolation probes and 2.4 - 2.5 lines walked per record; a child of a foreign record is searched from its parent's position (usually a few lines back). Every answer is verified against the id in the line (section 6, W5).}

\p{\strong{Cost, measured} (prototype, variant A; the same decode run with the foreign references replaced by a constant, section 5.3): Init 0.27 s of CPU time for 0.96 M records (279 ns each; 53 \% of the whole decode); Std 0.52 s of CPU time for 1.85 M records (280 ns each; 56 \% of the whole decode); cedar 1.08 s of CPU time for 3.42 M records (315 ns each; 65 \% of the whole decode); mathlib 4.74 s of CPU time for 15.33 M records (309 ns each; 49 \% of the whole decode). The search itself is probably not what costs (replacing its per-byte reads by 8-byte steps changed nothing measurable); the likely cost is memory latency: the line, the range rows, and above all the shared children of the record, scattered over the export region, which the eager design pays on the loader thread when it promotes. Not profiled.}

\p{\strong{Assessment.}}

\ul{
\li{\strong{A (prototype)} needs nothing shared and nothing concurrent, and costs as much as the worker's own range or more (49 - 65 \% of the decode).}
\li{\strong{B} would parse 2.2 - 2.8 times fewer records (mathlib 8.36 M instead of 18.29 M: about 2.6 s of CPU time less at the measured unit cost) and gives the records shared ids again, which every table keyed by shared id (facts, warm sessions) likes. It needs what the store does not have today: a region written by several workers (a compare-and-set slot per ordinal, nodes published before the slot; 32 bytes per node: mathlib 268 MB, less than the 317 MB of the eager loader's 9.9 M promoted copies). The binding guard of section 6 is what makes such a node independent of the worker that builds it. Worth building only together with the rest.}
\li{\strong{C} is no option: the ranges that some later proof enters hold 83 - 94 \% of the records, so "on first use" means parsing nearly everything a second time, serialised on a lock per range.}
\li{A second pass of the loader cannot help: it would have to parse the ranges to learn the references.}
\li{The loader's own promotion on demand (a shared record refers into a range: 1.7 - 2.7 \% of the records left) uses the same search and is in the measured load times.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3. What the loader's serial part becomes (question b)}

\p{\strong{It still looks at every line.} The loader learns the kind of a segment only at its declaration line, and the name and level records that lie inside ranges (25 - 45 \% of all name records) are its own business, so it needs the start and the first bytes of every line. What it saves is the tokenising of the structure lines of the ranges and everything the sink does with them (segment record, block record, promotion). The number of expression lines of a segment gives the range without tokenising them: the range is the last \code{\verb~|V - T~} expression lines, and "V is the id of the last expression line" is a count of line starts.}

\p{Measured on one thread (JVM C2, warm; \code{\verb~|X5Tool scan~} / \code{\verb~|reader~}; section 5.1): copying the mapped file through the reader's buffer 18 - 30 GB/s; one newline search per line 4.4 - 5.1 GB/s; the class of every line from its first bytes plus the newline search \strong{4.0 - 4.3 GB/s (13 ns per line)}; the template scanner on every line (reader alone, null sink) 1.7 - 2.0 GB/s; the lazy reader alone 2.3 - 2.6 GB/s. A \code{\verb~|memchr~}-speed pass exists only for the copy; the per-line floor in this code base is the eight-byte-step newline search, which the classification (one load and compare per line) raises by 10 \%.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Loader thread, lazy}\th{bytes}\th{records}}
\tr{\td{pass one: line ends and classes}\td{all (mathlib 6.33 GB: 1.47 s measured)}\td{none}}
\tr{\td{names and levels}\td{their lines}\td{all (mathlib 4.3 M)}}
\tr{\td{expression records}\td{prefix lines, lines of eager units, leaf lines of ranges: 23 - 31 \% of the bytes}\td{18 - 28 \% (mathlib 29.6 M of 107.0 M)}}
\tr{\td{declaration lines}\td{all}\td{all (mathlib 721,845 units)}}
\tr{\td{promotion on demand}\td{random access into ranges}\td{1.7 - 2.7 \% of the records left (mathlib 2.07 M)}}
\tr{\td{not done any more}\td{-}\td{72 - 82 \% of the segment records, all block records (mathlib 78.2 M, 361 MB), 79 \% of the promotions (mathlib 9.9 M -> 2.1 M), the writes of \code{\verb~|loc~} for the ranges}}
}

\p{\strong{Expected load time of mathlib from the published profile} (\src{rlvm-load/README.md} "Wave 7", \src{rlvm-store/README.md} "Wave 7"; native \code{\verb~|-O3~}, final figure 12.40 s, \code{\verb~|work/status/rlvm-load.log~}): reader 22 \% = 2.7 s, kernel (page faults) 11 \% = 1.4 s, \code{\verb~|Loader.exprApp~} 12 \% = 1.5 s, promotion 10 \% = 1.2 s, \code{\verb~|NodeCtx.writeApp~} 8 \% = 1.0 s, names 7 \% = 0.9 s, block writer and \code{\verb~|ref~} 8 \% = 1.0 s, the rest 22 \% = 2.7 s. Lazy: reader 1.6 s (pass one) + 0.31 x 2.7 s = 2.4 s; page faults of the input stay, those of blocks, memo and three quarters of \code{\verb~|loc~} go: about 1.0 s; names 0.9 s; \code{\verb~|exprApp~} x 0.28 = 0.4 s; node writes x 0.82 (31.7 M instead of 38.7 M export nodes) = 0.8 s; promotion from the file, 2.07 M records at 0.3 us = 0.6 s; block writer 0; the rest x 0.45 = 1.2 s. \strong{Sum 7.3 s, 59 \% of the eager load}; with pass one on a scanner thread 5.7 s. The prototype measures the same ratio (section 5.1): 0.57 - 0.64 on Init, Std and cedar, 0.60 on mathlib.}

\p{\strong{Why not a fifth.} F1 counted records. The sink's time is not proportional to them: names, levels, declaration rows and page faults do not shrink, the reader has a per-line floor, and the 18 - 28 \% of the records that stay are the shared ones, whose nodes are the expensive ones to write (derived facts, children in a region far larger than the caches).}
}

\subtree{
\title{4. The prototype}

\p{Private copies of \code{\verb~|rlvm-export~}, \code{\verb~|rlvm-store~}, \code{\verb~|rlvm-load~} (sources of the jars published at 14:51Z / 14:52Z) under \code{\verb~|work/prof/X5/~}, built by \code{\verb~|work/prof/X5/build.sh~} (javac into \code{\verb~|work/prof/X5/classes~}, against the installed \code{\verb~|rlvm-level~} / \code{\verb~|rlvm-nat~} jars; nothing is installed). 2,950 lines new, 350 lines changed in \code{\verb~|Loader~}.}

\table{
\tr{\th{File}\th{What}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|rlvm-export/.../LazyRead.java~} (556 lines), \code{\verb~|LazySink.java~}, \code{\verb~|LazyRestartException.java~}}\td{the lazy reader and its sink protocol (started by the first half of this scout; completed: visibility in table mode, class verification)}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|rlvm-export/.../Lines.java~}}\td{line classes from first bytes; \code{\verb~|RangeScanner~} / \code{\verb~|RangeOut~}: the template scanner for workers}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|rlvm-export/.../RangeFile.java~}, \code{\verb~|LazyRanges.java~}}\td{one record of a range by id; the range table}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|rlvm-export/.../IdSpace.java~} (+35 lines)}\td{\code{\verb~|show / showAll~}: which ids are visible while the lines of a segment are parsed out of file order}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|rlvm-load/.../Loader.java~} (+350 lines)}\td{\code{\verb~|implements LazySink~}: ranges, leaves, promotion on demand from the file, binding guard}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|rlvm-store/.../RangeDecoder.java~} (602 lines)}\td{worker: range -> overlay nodes (as \code{\verb~|ProofBlocks.decode~}), foreign records, checks W1 - W5}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|rlvm-store/.../X5Digest.java~}, \code{\verb~|rlvm-load/.../tools/X5Tool.java~}}\td{structural digest; \code{\verb~|load~}, \code{\verb~|reader~}, \code{\verb~|scan~}, \code{\verb~|decode~}, \code{\verb~|run~}, \code{\verb~|diff~}, \code{\verb~|explain~}}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|sim/LazySim.java~}, \code{\verb~|sim/LazySim2.java~}}\td{the two simulations on the published reader}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|measure.sh~}, \code{\verb~|measure-mathlib.sh~}, \code{\verb~|tools/summ.py~}, \code{\verb~|runs/*.txt~}, \code{\verb~|data/*.txt~}}\td{measurement windows and their raw output}}
}

\p{What the prototype is not: it is a batch load (the workers decode after the load, so nothing is published concurrently); \code{\verb~|opaque~} records are loaded eagerly; the theorem's value word in the store is 0 for a range (the range table is beside the store); no engine runs on it. Its store is compared with the eager store instead (section 5.5).}
}

\subtree{
\title{5. Measurements}

\p{Method: \code{\verb~|bench/quiet.py -- work/prof/X5/measure.sh CORPUS HEAP~} (one window per corpus; 8 performance cores; containers of other agents parked since 16:04Z), JVM (GraalVM CE 25, C2, \code{\verb~|-XX:+UseSerialGC -XX:-UseJVMCICompiler~}, the launcher's flags), one process per step, 3 or 4 passes, \strong{median of the warm passes} (the first is dropped). Windows 16:14Z - 16:18Z (\code{\verb~|runs/{init,std,cedar}-q5.txt~}); the windows before 16:04Z (\code{\verb~|*-q3.txt~}, \code{\verb~|*-q4.txt~}) were contended and are kept only as a second sample of the single-threaded numbers (lazy load: Init 0.307 / 0.318 s, Std 0.503 / 0.574 s, cedar 0.805 s). mathlib: \strong{one} run (\code{\verb~|runs/mathlib-final.txt~}), one process, 13 GB heap, after a warm-up on Init in the same process, one pass per mode, an explicit collection in front of every load (collector time including that collection: 339 ms up to the end of the eager load, 151 ms more up to the end of the lazy load).}

\subtree{
\title{5.1 Load phase alone (one thread, wall = CPU)}

\table{
\tr{\th{}\th{Init}\th{Std}\th{cedar}\th{mathlib}}
\tr{\td{input}\td{347.8 MB}\td{584.3 MB}\td{904.7 MB}\td{6326.1 MB}}
\tr{\td{copy through the buffer alone}\td{0.011 s}\td{0.025 s}\td{0.030 s}\td{0.349 s}}
\tr{\td{one newline search per line}\td{0.077 s (4.5 GB/s)}\td{0.133 s (4.4 GB/s)}\td{0.190 s (4.8 GB/s)}\td{1.229 s (5.1 GB/s)}}
\tr{\td{line ends + class of every line (pass one)}\td{0.086 s (4.0 GB/s)}\td{0.145 s (4.0 GB/s)}\td{0.218 s (4.2 GB/s)}\td{1.468 s (4.3 GB/s)}}
\tr{\td{reader alone (null sink), eager}\td{0.205 s (1692 MB/s)}\td{0.336 s (1739 MB/s)}\td{0.493 s (1833 MB/s)}\td{3.148 s (2010 MB/s)}}
\tr{\td{reader alone (null sink), lazy}\td{0.147 s (2366 MB/s)}\td{0.254 s (2300 MB/s)}\td{0.387 s (2341 MB/s)}\td{2.441 s (2592 MB/s)}}
\tr{\td{\strong{load, eager} (reader + loader + store)}\td{\strong{0.519 s} (670 MB/s)}\td{\strong{0.929 s} (629 MB/s)}\td{\strong{1.429 s} (633 MB/s)}\td{\strong{10.637 s} (595 MB/s)}}
\tr{\td{\strong{load, lazy ranges}}\td{\strong{0.318 s} (1094 MB/s)}\td{\strong{0.532 s} (1098 MB/s)}\td{\strong{0.915 s} (989 MB/s)}\td{\strong{6.351 s} (996 MB/s)}}
\tr{\td{lazy / eager}\td{0.61 (-39 \%, 1.63 times faster)}\td{0.57 (-43 \%, 1.75 times faster)}\td{0.64 (-36 \%, 1.56 times faster)}\td{0.60 (-40 \%, 1.67 times faster)}}
\tr{\td{loader + store share (load minus reader alone), eager -> lazy}\td{0.314 -> 0.171 s}\td{0.593 -> 0.278 s}\td{0.935 -> 0.528 s}\td{7.489 -> 3.910 s}}
\tr{\td{lazy load without pass one (if a scanner thread does it)}\td{0.232 s}\td{0.387 s}\td{0.697 s}\td{4.883 s}}
}

\p{The warmed-up JVM loads mathlib eagerly in 10.6 s; the native image needs 12.4 s for the same store (its reader's \code{\verb~|Mem.u64~} and \code{\verb~|Scanner.num~} are out-of-line calls), and a cold JVM process 14 - 15 s (\src{rlvm-load/README.md}). The ratio lazy / eager is what the next sections use.}
}

