}
}

\subtree{
\title{5.5 Agreement with the eager loader}

\p{\code{\verb~|X5Tool diff~} loads a file eagerly and lazily and compares, for EVERY constant, a structural digest of its type and of its value (the proof decoded from its block, respectively parsed from its range with its foreign records): kind, constants and structures by name and bound / unbound, level lists by content, literals by value, children by digest; plus the derived facts of every root (flags, free-variable mask, \code{\verb~|depMax~}). Term ids are not compared (they differ by construction: the lazy store has fewer promoted copies), nor are level-list ids (the store interns its \code{\verb~|lpSet~} unions in the same table, so their numbering depends on which nodes it writes).}

\table{
\tr{\th{Input}\th{Files}\th{Loaded by both, all digests and root facts equal}\th{Fail alike (same exception and message)}\th{Lazy restart (eager decides)}\th{Different}}
\tr{\td{Init / Std / cedar / mathlib}\td{4}\td{59,979 / 103,885 / 151,772 / 739,167 constants}\td{-}\td{0}\td{\strong{0}}}
\tr{\td{230 arena tests}\td{230}\td{224}\td{6}\td{0}\td{\strong{0}}}
\tr{\td{mutants (tutorial, bugs, other, mdata, prelude)}\td{17,775}\td{12,671}\td{4,795}\td{309}\td{\strong{0}}}
}

\p{122,179 ranges in 3,584 of the 18,005 small files were really left in the file and parsed back. Restarts by cause: 197 a constant named in a range is declared later (or has the wrong number of levels), 87 the ids of names / levels lose their order after expression lines of the segment were classified (mostly \code{\verb~|drop_record~}), 13 a range refers to a proof block with unresolved records, 9 a projection of a structure that is declared later, 3 a name id used before its definition inside a range. Per mutation kind: \code{\verb~|work/prof/X5/data/x5diff-mutants.txt~}. \strong{Without the binding guard 14 \code{\verb~|reorder_decl~} mutants load to a different store} (\code{\verb~|x5diff-mutants-noguard.txt~}): a \code{\verb~|CONST~} first written inside a proof is unbound there, and the eager loader binds its promoted copy when a later unit refers to it; the lazy path, which writes the leaf once, did not.}

\p{The simulation's view of the same corpora by mutation kind (\code{\verb~|data/lazysim2-mutants.tsv~}, 18,005 files, 176,230 \code{\verb~|thm~} / \code{\verb~|opaque~} units, 174,971 of them lazy): 141 units whose value is not the last record of its segment (2 in the unmutated arena tests and inherited by their mutants; 15 in \code{\verb~|swap_decl_root~}, 30 in \code{\verb~|wrap_mdata~}), 2 with the type after the value (\code{\verb~|swap_decl_root~}), 117 with a prefix record that the type does not reach (\code{\verb~|drop_decl~} 43, \code{\verb~|reorder_decl~} 41, \code{\verb~|swap_child~} 18, \code{\verb~|change_binder_type~} 7, \code{\verb~|swap_decl_root~} 6, \code{\verb~|change_decl_kind~} 2), 999 whose value is an earlier record; all 880 \code{\verb~|renumber_ids~} files and 2,238 of the 2,802 \code{\verb~|wrap_mdata~} files leave dense mode; 2,107 files are the reader's rejection.}
}

\subtree{
\title{5.6 End to end with 8 workers: wall = max(serial loader, checking)}

\p{The prototype has no engine, so this is arithmetic on measured terms. Under pipelining a pass costs max(serial loader, checking); lazy ranges shorten the first term and lengthen the second by the extra decode CPU divided by the parallelism of the check phase. The published terms: load of the native image, one thread (\code{\verb~|work/status/p7.log~} 15:47Z: Std 1.1 s, cedar 1.6 s; \code{\verb~|rlvm-load.log~} 15:19Z: mathlib 12.40 s); check phase of the machine with 8 workers on quiet cores (P7, 15:47Z: Std 1.85 s at 6.3 times one worker, cedar 2.26 s at 7.1 times; mathlib 42.3 s wall and 206 s CPU for the whole run, machine owners 13:30Z, \src{rlvm-driver/README.md}). The lazy terms: the measured ratio of the load and the measured extra decode CPU of section 5.3 (JVM; one decode per proof).}

\table{
\tr{\th{8 workers, pipelined}\th{Std}\th{cedar}\th{mathlib}}
\tr{\td{load, eager, native \code{\verb~|-O3~}, one thread (published)}\td{1.1 s}\td{1.6 s}\td{12.4 s}}
\tr{\td{load, lazy = eager x the measured ratio}\td{0.63 s (x 0.57)}\td{1.02 s (x 0.64)}\td{7.40 s (x 0.60)}}
\tr{\td{checking today: wall of the check phase (CPU / wall)}\td{1.85 s (6.3)}\td{2.26 s (7.1)}\td{42.30 s (4.9)}}
\tr{\td{extra decode CPU with lazy ranges (measured, JVM)}\td{+0.69 s}\td{+1.34 s}\td{+6.93 s}}
\tr{\td{- as wall clock at that parallelism}\td{+0.11 s}\td{+0.19 s}\td{+1.41 s}}
\tr{\td{\strong{wall today: eager = max(load, check)}}\td{\strong{1.85 s} (check-bound)}\td{\strong{2.26 s} (check-bound)}\td{\strong{42.30 s} (check-bound)}}
\tr{\td{\strong{wall today: lazy}}\td{\strong{1.96 s} (+5.9 \%)}\td{\strong{2.45 s} (+8.3 \%)}\td{\strong{43.71 s} (+3.3 \%)}}
\tr{\td{lazy starts to win when the check phase is below}\td{0.99 s (1.9 times faster than today)}\td{1.41 s (1.6 times faster than today)}\td{10.99 s (3.9 times faster than today)}}
\tr{\td{largest possible gain (check phase below the lazy load)}\td{1.10 -> 0.63 s (-43 \%)}\td{1.60 -> 1.02 s (-36 \%)}\td{12.40 -> 7.40 s (-40 \%)}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Today lazy ranges make every corpus slower} (+6 \%, +8 \%, +3 \%): checking is the larger term, the load's share of the wall clock is already gone through pipelining, and the workers get more to do.}
\li{They start to win when the check phase drops below the eager load minus the extra decode time: 1.9 times faster checking than today on Std, 1.6 times on cedar, 3.9 times on mathlib. From there the gain grows until the check phase is as short as the lazy load; the most that can ever be gained is the load's own reduction, 36 - 43 \% of the wall clock of a loader-bound run.}
\li{Against the eager loader with scanner threads (\code{\verb~|-X scan-threads=2~}: mathlib 14.4 -> 11.3 s wall before wave 7, -21 \%) half of that largest gain is already available without any change; lazy ranges could use scanner threads too (pass one, 23 - 27 \% of the lazy load, off the sink thread: last row of table 5.1), which was estimated, not built.}
\li{With \code{\verb~|-X passes=N~} in its check-only form (\code{\verb~|passes-mode=recheck~}) the load is not part of a measured pass: lazy ranges then cost the extra decode in every pass and gain nothing, unless the packed form of a range is kept after its first decode.}
\li{At the CPU cost of the fastest competitor on mathlib (about 18 s wall with 4 threads, 62 s CPU): with 8 workers at today's parallelism of 4.9 the check phase would be 12.7 s, the eager run 12.7 s and the lazy run max(7.4, (62 + 6.9) / 4.9) = 14.1 s; at a parallelism of 7 (what P7 measures on Std and cedar) the check phase would be 8.9 s, the eager run 12.4 s (loader-bound; about 10 s with scanner threads) and the lazy run max(7.4, (62 + 6.9) / 7) = 9.8 s.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. Soundness: what is verified, by whom, and what happens to a file that is not exporter output}

\p{The eager path stays as it is and decides every file the lazy path does not take. The lazy path can only ever contribute an ACCEPT; the argument has to show: \em{lazy ACCEPT implies eager ACCEPT}.}

\subtree{
\title{6.1 What the loader thread verifies before it leaves anything in the file (per unit, at the declaration line)}

\p{L1. The reader is in the state "ids are 0, 1, 2, ... in file order" for names and levels and "dense without a table" for expressions, since the start of the file (\code{\verb~|IdSpace.denseInOrder()~} / \code{\verb~|dense~}). Lost once = lazy mode ends for the rest of the file; lost after expression lines of the open segment were classified = restart.}

\p{L2. Every line of the segment starts like a name, level, leaf, structure, projection or declaration record (\code{\verb~|Lines.classify~}: the first 8 bytes; for \code{\verb~|{"ie":N,~} the 8 bytes after the number). Any other first bytes (\code{\verb~|mdata~}, whitespace, another key order, CRLF, a blank line): lazy mode ends, the open segment is parsed eagerly in file order.}

\p{L3. Every line the loader parses itself is what its first bytes say: a NAME / LEVEL line binds exactly one name / level and nothing else; a run of n expression-class lines binds exactly n expression ids and no name or level; a leaf-class line inside a range gives exactly one leaf record (the sink refuses a structure record there). Otherwise restart. (Needed because the names and levels of a segment are read ahead of its expression lines; with L3 that reordering is not observable, and "defined before use" stays exact: while an expression line is parsed, exactly the names and levels of the lines before it are visible, \code{\verb~|IdSpace.show~}.)}

\p{L4. The declaration line is a \code{\verb~|thm~} in template form, its value id V is the id of the LAST expression-class line of the segment (first id of the segment + number of expression-class lines - 1), its type id T is smaller than V. Else the unit is loaded eagerly.}

\p{L5. Binding guard, after the theorem's name is claimed: every record of the range that names something is resolved NOW: the CONST leaves are bound with the right number of levels, the literal leaves have their prerequisites, every projection names a declared structure (\code{\verb~|LoadTarget.unresolvedRefs~}, \code{\verb~|isUnresolved~}, \code{\verb~|isDeclared~} in the prototype). Else restart.}

\p{What the loader has then parsed, exactly as the eager loader parses it: all names and levels, the expression records with id <= T (the prefix; the usual mark / sweep: reached by the type -> shared, not reached -> the unit's proof block, which then is ownerless), the leaf records of the range, the declaration line. What it has NOT looked at beyond their first bytes and their line ends: the structure lines of the range.}
}

\subtree{
\title{6.2 What the worker verifies when it parses a range (\code{\verb~|RangeDecoder~}), before the unit can be accepted}

\p{W1. The range's bytes end with a newline and split into lines each of which is, for the same classifier and the reader's own template scanner: a name line, a level line, a leaf line (these three were parsed completely by the loader, L3), or an application / lambda / forall / let / projection \strong{in exact template form, byte for byte}. Anything else: restart.}

\p{W2. The k-th expression line of the range carries the id \code{\verb~|first + k~} (so the ids of the range are dense, in order, not repeated), and the number of expression lines is the number the loader counted.}

\p{W3. Every reference of a structure record goes to an expression id smaller than its own (defined before use, as the reader requires); every binder name and structure name is a name id smaller than the number of names defined before that line (names before the range + name lines of the range seen so far).}

\p{W4. A projection index is at most 2^31 - 1 (the eager loader declines above it); the projection was announced by the loader (its structure name translated on the loader thread).}

\p{W5. A reference to an earlier record without a location (a "foreign" record, in the range of an earlier theorem) is served by parsing that one line: it is found by id inside the byte range the loader recorded, starts after a newline, is in template form, carries exactly that id, refers only backward. A reference to a record of a proof block the loader wrote (ownerless blocks, eager theorems, opaques) decodes the block, and restarts if the block has an unresolved record (\code{\verb~|ProofBlocks.hasUnresolved~}): the eager loader would have promoted the record and bound it again for this unit.}
}

\subtree{
\title{6.3 Why the result is the eager loader's}

\ol{
\li{\em{Same records.} By L1-L3 and W1-W3 for every range, the eager reader reads the same file in dense mode and delivers the same records with the same ordinals; nothing it would reject or decline is present in a parsed range (W1-W4 are the reader's and loader's own conditions on such lines; any line that needs the general JSON path restarts instead of being interpreted).}
\li{\em{Same split.} With dense ids in file order a record refers only to smaller ids. So nothing reachable from the type T has an id above T, and every record of \verb~|(~T, V\verb~|]~ is unmarked for the eager loader: its block is exactly the unreached records of the prefix plus the range. The lazy path keeps the unreached prefix records in a block and the range in the file; the value is record V in both.}
\li{\em{Same promotions.} A shared record of a later unit that refers into a range (a later declaration's type that uses a proof record, a definition that shares a subterm with an earlier proof) is the eager loader's promotion: the lazy loader parses the record and what it reaches among records without a location from the file at that moment (W5's checks) and writes the same shared nodes. It promotes less only because it does not see the references that proof records make; those are the foreign records of W5, which the worker builds as overlay nodes.}
\li{\em{Same bindings.} The eager loader binds a block record when the theorem is declared and a promoted copy when it promotes it. By L5 every name-bearing record of a range is bound at the theorem's declaration; a name is declared once (a second declaration is the loader's rejection); so the record denotes the same constant whenever and for whomever it is materialised. Without L5 this fails: 14 \code{\verb~|reorder_decl~} mutants (section 5.5).}
\li{\em{ACCEPT needs every range parsed.} A unit is accepted only after its worker parsed its range (W1-W5 hold for it); a run is accepted only if every unit is. So at ACCEPT every range of the file has passed W1-W5, the loader finished without a restart, and by 1-4 the eager store is isomorphic to what was checked (per constant: the same type and value up to term ids). A foreign line read by an earlier-finishing worker belongs to a range whose own worker validates it before the run can be accepted.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6.4 The rules that make it exact}

\p{R1. \strong{Restart, never guess.} Any failure of L1 (after the fact), L3, L5, W1-W5, and any \code{\verb~|LazyRestartException~} of the loader's promotion on demand, ends the lazy run: every outcome obtained so far is discarded and the file is read again by the eager path (which then rejects, declines or accepts it as today).}

\p{R2. \strong{Only ACCEPT is final under lazy loading.} A REJECT or DECLINE reached while ranges are unparsed is not exact (an earlier bad line may sit in a range nobody parsed; a message and line number would differ; a loader rejection assumes the ids of the skipped ranges): the run is repeated eagerly. Failing runs pay one extra load; they already pay a second check of the failing unit in pipelined mode.}

\p{R3. \strong{Every range is parsed before ACCEPT}, also the range of a unit whose value an engine never asked for: the store keeps a "validated" bit per range, the board accepts only when all are set (a worker validates the rest at the end: tokenising alone runs at 1.7 GB/s per core).}

\p{R4. The export must not change during the run (it is read twice). The eager path has the same assumption for the one pass it makes; it is stated here because a change between the two reads is not detected.}
}

\subtree{
\title{6.5 Hostile and mutated files against the rule}

\table{
\tr{\th{File property}\th{What happens}}
\tr{\td{ids not dense / not in order (880 \code{\verb~|renumber_ids~}, sparse ids, ids >= 2^63)}\td{L1: never lazy, or lazy until the first such line}}
\tr{\td{ids not dense INSIDE a range (a structure line with another id, an id twice)}\td{W2: restart}}
\tr{\td{a shared record refers into a range; a later type or definition uses a proof record}\td{promotion on demand from the file (6.3.3); 1.7 - 2.7 \% of the records left on exporter output}}
\tr{\td{value is not the last record of its segment; type id >= value id; value is an earlier record}\td{L4: the unit is eager (141 + 2 units in the mutants; 52,306 theorems and opaques of mathlib have an earlier record as value and no range)}}
\tr{\td{prefix record not reached by the type (records written early, \code{\verb~|drop_decl~}, \code{\verb~|reorder_decl~}, \code{\verb~|swap_child~}: 117 units)}\td{the record goes to the unit's block as today; a range that refers to it decodes the block (W5)}}
\tr{\td{a proof names a constant that is declared later (\code{\verb~|reorder_decl~}, \code{\verb~|drop_decl~}, \code{\verb~|swap_name~}, \code{\verb~|forge_flag~})}\td{L5 / W5: restart (219 mutants)}}
\tr{\td{a structure line of a range uses a name or expression id defined later}\td{W3: restart (3 mutants)}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|mdata~}, reformatted JSON, CRLF, blank line}\td{L2: lazy mode ends at that line (\code{\verb~|wrap_mdata~}: 2,238 of 2,802 files leave it at their first \code{\verb~|mdata~} record; \code{\verb~|reformat_json~}: 875 of 880 files have no range at all)}}
\tr{\td{a line that starts like one kind and is another}\td{L3 (loader side), W1 (worker side): restart}}
\tr{\td{a record dropped or duplicated (\code{\verb~|drop_record~}, \code{\verb~|duplicate_record~})}\td{the reader's rejection on the loader thread, or restart when the id spaces lose their order after expression lines were classified (87)}}
\tr{\td{a segment above 1 GiB}\td{lazy mode ends there (the segment buffer is bounded); long lines are no problem (mathlib has one of 2.7 MB)}}
}

\p{Evidence (section 5.5): the prototype against the eager loader on 18,005 files and on Init, Std, cedar, mathlib.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. The design, for the owners}

\p{Terms as in \src{rlvm-load/README.md} ("What the loader does", "Pipelined loading"), \src{rlvm-store/README.md} (proof blocks, decoder, frontiers) and \src{rlvm-driver/README.md} ("Per unit", "Pipelined loading").}

\subtree{
\title{7.1 rlvm-export (reader)}

\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|LazyRead~} (prototype: \code{\verb~|work/prof/X5/rlvm-export/.../LazyRead.java~}, 556 lines): a third mode next to \code{\verb~|sequential~} and \code{\verb~|ParallelScan~}, chosen by \code{\verb~|ExportReader.Options.lazy~} when the sink is a \code{\verb~|LazySink~} and the input is mapped. Per segment: pass one classifies every line (\code{\verb~|Lines.classify~}) and finds its end, parses name and level lines at once; at the declaration line the rules L1 - L4 decide; pass two parses the prefix (or the whole segment) in file order; the range is announced with \code{\verb~|lazyBegin / lazySkip(n) / lazyProj(name, index) / lazyEnd(fileOffset, byteLength, count, ..)~}, its leaf lines are delivered as ordinary \code{\verb~|expr*~} calls.}
\li{\code{\verb~|IdSpace.show(n) / showAll()~}: visibility of names and levels while the expression lines of a segment are parsed after its name lines (exact "defined before use"), in dense and in table mode (the real store numbers names itself, so names are in table mode from the first special name on).}
\li{\code{\verb~|Lines.RangeScanner~} + \code{\verb~|RangeOut~} (public): the template scanner for the workers; \code{\verb~|RangeFile~}: one record of a range by id (interpolation on the bytes, then a short walk; every answer verified against the id in the line).}
\li{For \code{\verb~|ParallelScan~} (scan threads): the classification is context-free, so pass one can run on the helper threads per 4 MiB chunk (line starts + classes + the tokens of name / level / leaf / declaration lines and of the lines up to T); a segment that crosses a chunk border is tokenised completely, as today. Not built. It takes pass one (23 - 27 \% of the lazy load in the prototype) off the sink thread.}
\li{\code{\verb~|opaque~} records need the two ids from the general path (the template scanner has no \code{\verb~|opaque~} shape); the prototype loads them eagerly (0.4 - 1.3 \% of the ranges of section 1).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7.2 rlvm-load (loader)}

\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|Loader implements LazySink~} (prototype: 350 lines added to a private copy). State per range, nothing per record: \code{\verb~|LazyRanges~} = first ordinal, record count, file offset, byte length, names defined before, owner cid, structure count (28 bytes per range: mathlib 475,131 ranges = 13 MB) and, per projection record left in the file, (ordinal, store name id) (8 bytes; mathlib 18,818), because only the loader thread can translate a name id.}
\li{\code{\verb~|loc[ordinal] == 0~} now also means "left in the file" for an ordinal below the segment (today it never is 0 there). \code{\verb~|resolvePending~} / \code{\verb~|rootRef~}: \code{\verb~|l < 0~} promotes from a block as today, \code{\verb~|l == 0~} promotes from the file (\code{\verb~|lazyPromote~}: range by binary search over the first ordinals, line by \code{\verb~|RangeFile.find~}, children first, no recursion, same \code{\verb~|LoadTarget.app / lam / pi / let / proj~} calls as the sweep). \code{\verb~|loc~} of a range is not written at all (mathlib: 310 MB of the 428 MB stay untouched).}
\li{The leaf records of a range are written as shared nodes at the theorem's declaration (after the claim, so they bind as the block record would). The binding guard L5 is what makes this the eager loader's node.}
\li{The theorem's value: the range (new value word, 7.3) unless V is a leaf (then the shared term: 33 / 86 / 122 units on Init / Std / cedar).}
\li{New in \code{\verb~|LoadOptions~}: \code{\verb~|withLazyRanges(boolean)~} (off for \code{\verb~|withProofBlocks(false)~}), \code{\verb~|withLazyMinRecords(n)~}; \code{\verb~|LoadReport~}: ranges, bytes and records left, leaves, promotions from the file. \code{\verb~|ExportLoader~} lets \code{\verb~|LazyRestartException~} through as a third outcome ("read again eagerly"), not a verdict.}
\li{Pipelined mode: the range rows, the projection rows and \code{\verb~|loc~} have to be published with the frontier (today \code{\verb~|loc~} is the loader's private array): the workers read \code{\verb~|loc~} for every reference of a range to an earlier record. Rule for a reader on frontier n: a location above the export end of that frontier (a promotion made for a later unit) counts as 0, i.e. the record is parsed from the file. \code{\verb~|loc~} has to stop moving when it grows (chunks), or be handed over like the other published arrays.}
\li{\code{\verb~|MalformedExportException.line()~}: the loader derives the line from its record counters; with skipped records that still holds (ordinals advance), but an error inside a range has no loader line: by R2 the eager rerun reports it.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7.3 rlvm-store}

\ul{
\li{\strong{A range is a proof block whose records are still text.} \code{\verb~|ProofBlocks~} gets a second row kind: file offset, byte length, first ordinal, record count instead of chunk and position; \code{\verb~|DeclTable.value(cid)~} stays \code{\verb~|-(block + 1)~}. Then \code{\verb~|Overlay.blockRoot(block)~}, the block map, \code{\verb~|mark / resetTo / reset~}, \code{\verb~|nodeCount(block)~} and every consumer (\code{\verb~|rlvm-machine~} \code{\verb~|Decl~} / \code{\verb~|Reduce~}, \code{\verb~|rlvm-ref~} \code{\verb~|RefKernel~}, \code{\verb~|rlvm-admit~} \code{\verb~|StructuralChecks~} / \code{\verb~|OfficialOrder~} / \code{\verb~|EnvBuilder~}, the test kit) work unchanged; \code{\verb~|hasUnresolved(block)~} is false for a file block by L5. The prototype does exactly this inside the overlay (\code{\verb~|RangeDecoder.decode~} ends with \code{\verb~|commitBlock~}).}
\li{\code{\verb~|ProofBlocks.decode~} dispatches on the row kind to the range decoder (prototype: \code{\verb~|rlvm.store.RangeDecoder~}, 602 lines, same \code{\verb~|NodeCtx~} writers and the same sharing-flag rule as \code{\verb~|decode~}). It needs the mapped export (\code{\verb~|Store.attachExport(MemorySegment)~}), the published \code{\verb~|loc~}, the range and projection rows, and the template scanner: either \code{\verb~|rlvm-store~} depends on \code{\verb~|rlvm-export~} (as the prototype's private copy does; the store's own rule today is "nothing but the JDK"), or the scanner is handed in through a small interface.}
\li{Promotion from the file as a store operation (\code{\verb~|StoreBuilder.promoteRecord(kind, children..)~} setting \code{\verb~|F_SHARED~} like \code{\verb~|promote~} does; the prototype uses the plain appends, whose flags come from the open unit only).}
\li{Memory: no block bytes (mathlib 361 MB), no promotion memo (up to 313 MB), no sparse record index; the export region ends smaller (mathlib 38.7 M -> 31.7 M nodes). In exchange the export stays mapped and its pages are read a second time, by the workers (section 8).}
\li{Optional, for check-only passes (\code{\verb~|-X passes=N~}) and re-checks: keep the packed form of a range after its first decode (write the block bytes then), so that only the first decode pays for text.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7.4 rlvm-driver (P7)}

\ul{
\li{Nothing in \code{\verb~|Worker~}'s per-unit flow if the store hides ranges behind block indices.}
\li{\strong{Restart} (R1): a \code{\verb~|LazyRestartException~} from the loading thread or from any worker cancels the run like an \code{\verb~|Error~} in the loader does today (checking threads joined, outcomes discarded), then \code{\verb~|execute()~} runs once more with lazy ranges off. Statistic \code{\verb~|driver.lazy.restarts~}.}
\li{\strong{Verdict} (R2, R3): with lazy ranges only ACCEPT is recorded as final, and only when every range is validated; anything else reruns eagerly. This composes with the existing rule "nothing but an ACCEPT is recorded for a unit that started before the end of the load".}
\li{Scheduling: the frontier advances about 1.6 - 1.75 times faster, a theorem costs its worker 10 - 19 us more on average (section 5.3); \code{\verb~|-X scan-threads~} can take pass one.}
\li{\code{\verb~|-X lazy-ranges=on|off~} (default off until the gate is green in that mode), in \code{\verb~|rlvm-main~} / \code{\verb~|MainOptions~}.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7.5 If the worker price is the obstacle: let the scanner threads pack (not built, not measured)}

\p{Pass one is context-free, so a scanner thread can do per chunk what \code{\verb~|RangeDecoder~} does per range, without the store: tokenise the structure lines of a range and write them as block records whose references are still ordinals (own range: back distance; earlier record: its ordinal). The sink thread then appends the bytes of that block and never sees its records; a worker decodes an "ordinal block" with one \code{\verb~|loc~} read per reference to an earlier record, and fetches a foreign record by index from the earlier block instead of searching text. From the measured terms: the sink thread's time is the lazy load without pass one (last row of table 5.1); the worker's time for its own range is about the "without foreign records" row minus the tokenising (Init 0.09 s, Std 0.16 s, cedar 0.23 s: less than today's block decode, because the leaves are shared nodes already), plus the foreign records at a lower unit cost than from text. Every line is then parsed during the load, by the reader's own code and with its own verdicts, so the restart rule, the second read of the file and the dependence on the page cache disappear; the binding guard, the published \code{\verb~|loc~} and the foreign records remain. It needs two scanner threads that compete with the workers while the file loads (tokenising mathlib's ranges is 2.1 s of CPU time). If the loader ever is the floor, this variant should be costed before the text one is built.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{8. What was not established}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Native image.} Every time here is JVM C2 with the launcher's flags. The target platform's load is 12.4 s for mathlib (native \code{\verb~|-O3~}), where \code{\verb~|Mem.u64~} and \code{\verb~|Scanner.num~} are out-of-line calls (22 \% of the load is the reader): pass one and the workers' tokenising use the same primitives, so both sides of the trade may be slower there. The ratio lazy / eager is what should carry over; it was not measured.}
\li{\strong{Pipelined operation.} The prototype decodes after the load. Not measured: workers and loader running together (memory bandwidth: the loader writes the store while eight workers read 4.3 GB of text), the publication of \code{\verb~|loc~} and of the range table, the frontier rule for late promotions (7.2).}
\li{\strong{How often a proof is decoded in a real run.} Admission's structural checks and the engine call \code{\verb~|Overlay.blockRoot~} on the same worker overlay (\code{\verb~|WorkerContext.overlay~}), so normally once per unit; a session reset between the two, a re-check and every later pass of \code{\verb~|-X passes=N~} decode again. There is no counter. The worker price of section 5.3 is per decode.}
\li{\strong{The page cache.} The lazy run reads the export twice, the second time at random from eight threads, up to the end of the run. All measurements had the file in the page cache (the mathlib run was preceded by a \code{\verb~|cat~}). On the arena's 16 GB runner mathlib's 6.3 GB of file pages compete with the store; if they are reclaimed the workers wait for the disk. The eager loader drops the pages behind it (\code{\verb~|unloadMapped~}).}
\li{\strong{Scanner threads together with lazy ranges} (pass one on helper threads): estimated from the measured pass one, not built.}
\li{\strong{A table shared by the workers for foreign records} (variant B of section 2): counted, not built.}
\li{\strong{\code{\verb~|opaque~} units}: loaded eagerly by the prototype (0.4 - 1.3 \% of the ranges of the simulation).}
\li{\strong{mathlib}: one run, no repetition (a first attempt at 16:24Z measured nothing: the tool's newline micro-measurement looped on mathlib's 2.7 MB line and held the measurement lock for 270 s before it was killed; fixed and tested on a synthetic export with 3 MB lines, \code{\verb~|work/prof/X5/testdata/~}); con-leche: simulation only.}
\li{\strong{Files that change while they are read}, and exotic inputs beyond the 18,005 files (the generators of \code{\verb~|rlvm-testkit~} were not rerun with new mutation kinds aimed at ranges: a structure line with a foreign id, a range line with trailing bytes, a leaf-class line that is a structure record). The checks W1 - W3 and L3 are written for them and three of the 309 restarts come from W3, but a campaign that targets the range rule directly is owed before this could ship.}
}
}

